Ok. I don't think we should really bicker about this anymore. It
obviously isn't really a big deal anyway. But I do want to say a couple
more things.

>> The fact is, Nethack is a notoriously difficult game.
>
> I used to think that, but it's a long time now since Marvin showed
> that idea's just plain wrong.

I don't see how one guy being good at the game shows that the game is
not difficult. I only manage to ascend a relatively small fraction of my
characters; and no one else I know in meat-world has ever managed to
ascend any at all. Difficulty is clearly a subjective and relative
thing, but I didn't think I'd be challenged when I said Nethack is
notoriously difficult.


As for the rest of the stuff, about what's cheesy and what's not - let
me just clarify a couple of things. I wasn't saying those techniques
"needed" to be used, or that they were poor game design or anything like
that. All I meant was that those are some weirdly powerful techniques.
For example, there are a bunch of different ways to steal from shops,
such as teleporting out, digging out, jumping diagonally out, trapping
the shop keeper, and so on - but all of those will trigger the keystone
kops, and cause the shopkeeper to hunt you down and so on. But stealing
with pets is just as easy to do, and it has no negative consequences at
all. -- So, to me that sounds like a cheesy technique - but I'm not
going to start suggesting to people that they shouldn't do it.

The reason I said that what you said sounded contemptuous was a that you
implied that players should have grown out of using such a cheesy crutch
strategy. - At least, that's how I was interpreting what you said based
on things like "I thought quest-artifact-
wishing got abandoned in the 90s as unspeakably cheesy."

In any case, I don't think there is any significant argument going on
here. We're just describing things in different ways and so on. I just
wanted to clarify those couple of things.

